<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rogneda of Polotsk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 960 – c. 1000</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Rogneda Rogvolodovna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rogneda of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a> was the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rogvolod">Rogvolod</a>, ruler of Polotsk. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a>, Vladimir of Novgorod, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir the Great</a>, took Polotsk around 978, killed her father and brothers and forced her to marry him. She bore him several sons, including Iziaslav, ancestor of the princes of Polotsk, and is traditionally named as the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a>. She later became a nun.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rogneda-of-polotsk.jpg" alt="Rogneda of Polotsk" width="320" height="169" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rahva%C5%82od,_Rahnieda_(Po%C5%82acak)._%D0%A0%D0%B0%D0%B3%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B4,_%D0%A0%D0%B0%D0%B3%D0%BD%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%B0_(%D0%9F%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%B0%D1%86%D0%B0%D0%BA).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
